<h2>What assessors should look for</h2>

<ol>
<li>
<strong>Business fit</strong> – Do the selected technologies address the actual business problem and user needs?
</li>

<li>
<strong>Non-functional alignment</strong> – Can the technology meet reliability, security, scalability, resilience, performance and operational requirements?
</li>

<li>
<strong>Proportionality</strong> – Is the solution appropriately sized for the challenge, avoiding both over-engineering and under-engineering?
</li>

<li>
<strong>Options analysis</strong> – Were alternative technology options considered before making a decision?
</li>

<li>
<strong>Trade-offs</strong> – Are cost, complexity, operational burden and future support considerations documented?
</li>

<li>
<strong>Evidence-based decision-making</strong> – Is there evidence supporting claims that the selected technology can meet expected service demands?
</li>
</ol>

<h2>What separates a 3 from a 4 or 5</h2>

<p>
A score of <strong>3</strong> generally indicates that technology choices appear reasonable and are supported by documented rationale, but important assumptions remain unvalidated or evidence of non-functional suitability is incomplete.
</p>

<p>
A score of <strong>4</strong> requires clear evidence that the selected technology has been assessed against both business needs and non-functional requirements, with documented trade-offs and governance review.
</p>

<p>
A score of <strong>5</strong> requires strong evidence that technology decisions are evidence-based, proportionate and optimised for both current and future needs. The organisation can demonstrate why the chosen approach achieves the right balance between functionality, cost, complexity and risk.
</p>

<h2>Suggested evidence examples (not SAF-mandated artefacts)</h2>

<ul>
<li>Technology options appraisals</li>
<li>Architecture Decision Records (ADRs)</li>
<li>Non-functional requirements specifications</li>
<li>Performance and capacity models</li>
<li>Scalability assessments</li>
<li>Operational support assessments</li>
<li>Reliability and resilience analysis</li>
<li>Architecture review outputs</li>
<li>Solution Design Overview documents</li>
<li>Governance papers supporting technology decisions</li>
</ul>




